WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:02.235
-Hi, Jim.
-Hi, Jimmy.

00:00:02.235 --> 00:00:03.336
How are you?

00:00:03.336 --> 00:00:05.305
-You look great.
-You look really good too.

00:00:05.305 --> 00:00:07.907
I like this casual thing
you have going.

00:00:07.907 --> 00:00:09.209
-Oh, thank you.

00:00:09.209 --> 00:00:11.243
-You're welcome.

00:00:11.243 --> 00:00:16.148
-I always love seeing you and we
always talk about New York City,

00:00:16.148 --> 00:00:18.784
because I know
you're back in the city.

00:00:18.784 --> 00:00:21.354
-Yeah.
-And I want to say

00:00:21.354 --> 00:00:23.189
I heard this before,
right before the interview,

00:00:23.189 --> 00:00:26.525
but I didn't know
that you had COVID at one point.

00:00:26.525 --> 00:00:27.727
-Well, not everybody did.

00:00:27.727 --> 00:00:28.994
But I figure, you know,

00:00:28.994 --> 00:00:31.697
why not talk about it
with you on television?

00:00:31.697 --> 00:00:32.799
-Wow.
-Yeah.

00:00:32.799 --> 00:00:35.201
We had it.
Todd and I both had it early on.

00:00:35.201 --> 00:00:37.904
It was like middle of March.

00:00:37.904 --> 00:00:40.339
And we didn't know what --
I'm sorry.

00:00:40.339 --> 00:00:42.308
I'm putting the dog down.

00:00:42.308 --> 00:00:44.076
We didn't know what it was.

00:00:44.076 --> 00:00:45.745
We thought we had colds.

00:00:45.745 --> 00:00:49.348
And, then, it seemed less
likely, and then, finally,

00:00:49.348 --> 00:00:51.317
we lost our sense of smell
and taste and we were like --

00:00:51.317 --> 00:00:54.287
-You did?
-Yeah. Utterly.

00:00:54.287 --> 00:00:57.623
And it defied the descriptions
for me.

00:00:57.623 --> 00:01:01.861
I didn't realize how completely
taste and smell could be gone.

00:01:01.861 --> 00:01:04.297
And when you're in quarantine,
and there is really nothing

00:01:04.297 --> 00:01:07.267
to do but eat,
oh, my God.

00:01:07.267 --> 00:01:08.434
That was brutal.

00:01:08.434 --> 00:01:09.935
-So, you didn't feel like
eating anything

00:01:09.935 --> 00:01:11.637
or just like everything
was just gross?

00:01:11.637 --> 00:01:14.574
-Oh, no, I ate everything,
but I just didn't taste it.

00:01:14.574 --> 00:01:15.742
You know?

00:01:15.742 --> 00:01:18.311
It was the definition
of wasted calories.

00:01:18.311 --> 00:01:19.712
-Right, yeah.

00:01:19.712 --> 00:01:23.482
I'm so happy that you guys
are back to being healthy.

00:01:23.482 --> 00:01:24.884
-Yeah, me too.

00:01:24.884 --> 00:01:28.922
-How did you stay busy
once you felt better?

00:01:28.922 --> 00:01:31.557
-Well, I tried
other creative outlets

00:01:31.557 --> 00:01:34.793
because acting obviously
wasn't happening,

00:01:34.793 --> 00:01:37.330
and I took a painting class
for a while.

00:01:37.330 --> 00:01:40.567
I took a creative writing class
for a while.

00:01:40.567 --> 00:01:42.335
And everything petered out.

00:01:42.335 --> 00:01:46.172
I don't know if the truth is,
"Well, you're just an actor,

00:01:46.172 --> 00:01:48.207
and that's what you do
and that's what you should do,"

00:01:48.207 --> 00:01:51.344
or if it's just I really
don't have the see-through

00:01:51.344 --> 00:01:54.480
stick-to-it-ness for anything.

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:57.984
It's sort of embarrassing,
but, you know, here I am.

00:01:57.984 --> 00:01:59.318
-Everyone went through phases.

00:01:59.318 --> 00:02:03.589
I know you went through
a blond phase in quarantine.

00:02:03.589 --> 00:02:06.492
Look at this. Wow.
-I did.

00:02:06.492 --> 00:02:10.029
-I like it. What made you want
to bleach your hair?

00:02:10.029 --> 00:02:14.067
-Well, I had dealt
with bleached products --

00:02:14.067 --> 00:02:15.401
Who says bleached products?

00:02:15.401 --> 00:02:18.103
I had bleached my hair,
like a bottle job and stuff

00:02:18.103 --> 00:02:20.039
off and on in my 20s,

00:02:20.039 --> 00:02:23.509
and it had been so long
since I'd done it,

00:02:23.509 --> 00:02:25.945
for obvious reasons --
work and the show --

00:02:25.945 --> 00:02:27.813
and I kept thinking,
"I'm going to do it now

00:02:27.813 --> 00:02:30.350
that the show is over,
but I'm going to do it right.

00:02:30.350 --> 00:02:31.617
I'm going to have somebody do it
where it looks

00:02:31.617 --> 00:02:33.219
natural and whatever."

00:02:33.219 --> 00:02:35.521
And then quarantine hit,

00:02:35.521 --> 00:02:37.190
and I thought, "Well, I guess
I'm not doing it."

00:02:37.190 --> 00:02:39.191
And then about six weeks in
and we'd been sick,

00:02:39.191 --> 00:02:42.362
I was like, "Screw it.
Let's buy some crap."

00:02:42.362 --> 00:02:43.796
And Todd did it.

00:02:43.796 --> 00:02:46.599
-Yeah.
-It took about six hours.

00:02:46.599 --> 00:02:47.934
It burned my scalp.

00:02:47.934 --> 00:02:49.468
But you know what?

00:02:49.468 --> 00:02:50.970
Beauty is pain.

00:02:50.970 --> 00:02:52.971
Or a mediocre look.

00:02:52.971 --> 00:02:55.474
-There is that one minute,
right, where you go,

00:02:55.474 --> 00:02:56.775
"Am I going to fry
all my hair off?

00:02:56.775 --> 00:02:58.478
What's happening?"
-Yes.

00:02:58.478 --> 00:03:00.146
-"Let's wash it out
because it's not working.

00:03:00.146 --> 00:03:02.048
Something's happening."

00:03:02.048 --> 00:03:04.950
-You know this feeling
very well, obviously.

00:03:04.950 --> 00:03:09.655
-Unfortunately, I think
we have it in the control room.

00:03:09.655 --> 00:03:12.258
That's me.
-Oh, Jimmy, yes!

00:03:12.258 --> 00:03:13.726
Wait. What is this for?

00:03:13.726 --> 00:03:17.530
-This was for a movie
called "Factory Girl."

00:03:17.530 --> 00:03:21.800
-I remember seeing you
like this. Absolutely right.

00:03:21.800 --> 00:03:23.369
-I was in the neighborhood.
It was so --

00:03:23.369 --> 00:03:25.037
And at the time, I thought --

00:03:25.037 --> 00:03:28.574
You can take it off the screen
now. It's just terrible.

00:03:28.574 --> 00:03:30.209
They kind of left it up
a little bit too long there.

00:03:30.209 --> 00:03:31.644
-Hold it. Hold it.

00:03:31.644 --> 00:03:34.047
-Someone up there is like,
"Yeah, I'll get him."

00:03:34.047 --> 00:03:36.716
I thought like,
"Oh, I can do the blo--"

00:03:36.716 --> 00:03:38.851
My character had blond hair.

00:03:38.851 --> 00:03:40.385
I go, "I can do that."

00:03:40.385 --> 00:03:43.256
And it just came out
completely orange and it was --

00:03:43.256 --> 00:03:45.157
-I don't think
it looks that bad.

00:03:45.157 --> 00:03:48.093
I say it because it doesn't look
that different from mine.

00:03:48.093 --> 00:03:50.163
So, looks pretty good.

00:03:50.163 --> 00:03:51.897
[ Laughter ]

00:03:51.897 --> 00:03:53.299
-I like the way it is now.

00:03:53.299 --> 00:03:56.202
It kind of looks much
more professional.

00:03:56.202 --> 00:03:59.038
-Yeah, they "fixed" it.
-Ah.

00:03:59.038 --> 00:04:01.708
-I now have a wonderful colorist
here in New York,

00:04:01.708 --> 00:04:03.175
which I never needed before,

00:04:03.175 --> 00:04:07.012
but I threw on a mask
and was like, "I need help.

00:04:07.012 --> 00:04:09.381
Please." Oh, God.

00:04:09.381 --> 00:04:11.617
-I remember when we were talking
when you were on the show

00:04:11.617 --> 00:04:13.119
and we showed a photo of you

00:04:13.119 --> 00:04:14.520
when you first got
to New York City,

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:18.458
and I think you were
blond then as well.

00:04:18.458 --> 00:04:20.126
Yeah. That's it.
-See? I told you.

00:04:20.126 --> 00:04:25.130
My 20s were a time of bleached
hair and sleeping on couches.

00:04:25.130 --> 00:04:26.532
Yes, that was my first trip
to New York.

00:04:26.532 --> 00:04:30.069
-Look at you, Times Square.
-That is Times Square, isn't it?

00:04:30.069 --> 00:04:31.537
Oh, my God.
Yeah.

00:04:31.537 --> 00:04:32.971
-I know, I know.
I'm sorry.

00:04:32.971 --> 00:04:34.407
Take that photo off, too.
Please.

00:04:34.407 --> 00:04:37.009
Making us reminisce too much.

00:04:37.009 --> 00:04:38.344
My gosh.

00:04:38.344 --> 00:04:41.847
I haven't seen you since
"The Big Bang Theory" ended.

00:04:41.847 --> 00:04:44.016
-Yeah.
-It was the first time for you,

00:04:44.016 --> 00:04:46.018
probably, in a long time
that you weren't doing it.

00:04:46.018 --> 00:04:48.554
Was it weird?

00:04:48.554 --> 00:04:50.055
-Yeah, it was weird.

00:04:50.055 --> 00:04:54.727
It's been really hard to decide
what is "Big Bang" being gone

00:04:54.727 --> 00:04:57.263
and what's being now stuck in
this weird time of quarantine,

00:04:57.263 --> 00:04:59.431
like, the whole world
is in this weird time.

00:04:59.431 --> 00:05:02.902
But, yeah, the weirdest part
for me was the way --

00:05:02.902 --> 00:05:04.270
and I'm sorry
this sounds morbid --

00:05:04.270 --> 00:05:08.674
but it reminds me of when
someone dies or a pet passes

00:05:08.674 --> 00:05:11.677
and it feels so monumental
in the moment and you think,

00:05:11.677 --> 00:05:12.912
"Well, this will never end."

00:05:12.912 --> 00:05:14.514
And then one day
you kind of look up

00:05:14.514 --> 00:05:17.516
and you're like,
"Oh, nine months have gone by.

00:05:17.516 --> 00:05:19.986
Okay. I guess life carries on."

00:05:19.986 --> 00:05:21.721
I mean, not that it wasn't --
it wasn't --

00:05:21.721 --> 00:05:25.391
I mean, it was bittersweet
when it ended, but it was fine.

00:05:25.391 --> 00:05:26.826
-Yeah.

00:05:26.826 --> 00:05:28.261
A lot of people were talking
on the Internet

00:05:28.261 --> 00:05:31.798
of how they think Sheldon
would handle the pandemic.

00:05:31.798 --> 00:05:34.066
What do you think he'd be doing?

00:05:34.066 --> 00:05:35.400
-He was built for this.

00:05:35.400 --> 00:05:38.570
I mean, this is the moment
he was waiting for.

00:05:38.570 --> 00:05:41.174
I was saying earlier we had
an entire episode,

00:05:41.174 --> 00:05:42.608
which I didn't think about
until recently,

00:05:42.608 --> 00:05:45.944
where he did like a Shell-bot
where he had a video screen

00:05:45.944 --> 00:05:48.547
on a remote control
wheelie thing.

00:05:48.547 --> 00:05:50.215
That was when people
still needed

00:05:50.215 --> 00:05:51.517
to get together in groups.

00:05:51.517 --> 00:05:53.719
And so he would just send that
out and sit in his room.

00:05:53.719 --> 00:05:56.322
-Yeah, he didn't
have to see humans, yeah.

00:05:56.322 --> 00:06:00.326
-Yeah. Don't touch me.
Don't sneeze on me.

00:06:00.326 --> 00:06:02.895
So I guess he'd be fine.

00:06:02.895 --> 00:06:04.363
-You've been doing a lot
of great things since then.

00:06:04.363 --> 00:06:07.132
You were nominated for an Emmy
this year for "Hollywood."

00:06:07.132 --> 00:06:09.068
Congrats on that.

00:06:09.068 --> 00:06:10.236
That was great, by the way.

00:06:10.236 --> 00:06:11.704
I loved "Hollywood."
-Thank you.

00:06:11.704 --> 00:06:13.605
-How was the virtual
Emmy experience?

00:06:13.605 --> 00:06:15.074
What was that like?

00:06:15.074 --> 00:06:18.044
-So bizarre.
-It's weird, right?

00:06:18.044 --> 00:06:19.779
-So, you didn't
have to wear a tux,

00:06:19.779 --> 00:06:22.048
but I had a tux,
and I was like, "Why not?"

00:06:22.048 --> 00:06:24.783
So, me and Todd put on our tuxes
and got ready,

00:06:24.783 --> 00:06:26.919
and then we just walked
to the living room.

00:06:26.919 --> 00:06:30.655
And it was like,
this feels really odd.

00:06:30.655 --> 00:06:32.425
And then the whole thing,

00:06:32.425 --> 00:06:33.860
I think the strangest part
for me

00:06:33.860 --> 00:06:36.762
was right before the category
was announced that I was up for,

00:06:36.762 --> 00:06:39.365
they threw all the nominees
into a virtual waiting room,

00:06:39.365 --> 00:06:42.034
where they were like you
and I are right now on Zoom,

00:06:42.034 --> 00:06:44.736
and we could all kind of chat,
which I was like,

00:06:44.736 --> 00:06:46.605
"I don't -- We're all up
for the same award.

00:06:46.605 --> 00:06:48.774
This is weird."
But it ended up not being weird.

00:06:48.774 --> 00:06:50.709
It was actually very
humanizing and sweet.

00:06:50.709 --> 00:06:53.679
Then Yahya won, and it was like
somebody in the group had won.

00:06:53.679 --> 00:06:55.247
It was kind of really happy.

00:06:55.247 --> 00:06:57.616
But he finished his speech,
and we all applaud,

00:06:57.616 --> 00:07:00.352
and then suddenly,
literally from the Emmys,

00:07:00.352 --> 00:07:04.423
to leave it, you just go "leave
meeting" and then you were done.

00:07:04.423 --> 00:07:05.925
I was like --
-What? Wow.

00:07:05.925 --> 00:07:07.259
-It was on Zoom.

00:07:07.259 --> 00:07:08.494
It was so bizarre.

00:07:08.494 --> 00:07:10.363
-That is really --
-Kind of nice.

00:07:10.363 --> 00:07:13.566
I could eat and drink during it.
-Yeah, that's true.

00:07:13.566 --> 00:07:14.700
At commercial break,
you could actually walk

00:07:14.700 --> 00:07:16.035
around your house
and do whatever.

00:07:16.035 --> 00:07:17.970
-Yeah.
-I think it's kind of fun, too,

00:07:17.970 --> 00:07:21.573
having a conversation
with your fellow actors

00:07:21.573 --> 00:07:24.310
who you're "up against."

00:07:24.310 --> 00:07:26.345
-Well, it really gave
the lie to --

00:07:26.345 --> 00:07:28.347
We're not playing sports here.

00:07:28.347 --> 00:07:30.549
Nobody is beating anybody
at anything.

00:07:30.549 --> 00:07:32.384
We're all just doing our work,

00:07:32.384 --> 00:07:34.219
and if you happen
to get an award, great.

00:07:34.219 --> 00:07:37.623
But it's like, there's not
really a competition here.

00:07:37.623 --> 00:07:40.025
It's just a nice moment
of recognition.

00:07:40.025 --> 00:07:42.494
-We're actually all on
the same team, weirdly.

00:07:42.494 --> 00:07:43.829
-That's exactly right.

00:07:43.829 --> 00:07:46.599
-You're in another
Ryan Murphy film,

00:07:46.599 --> 00:07:47.833
"The Boys in the Band."

00:07:47.833 --> 00:07:49.969
I want to talk about this.

00:07:49.969 --> 00:07:51.437
We talked about the play
last time you were on,

00:07:51.437 --> 00:07:53.472
but you're getting
great reviews for this.

00:07:53.472 --> 00:07:56.042
Do you mind if you stick around?
I'll talk about that?

00:07:56.042 --> 00:07:57.876
-I need to go to the bathroom,
but maybe. Okay, yes, I'll stay.

00:07:57.876 --> 00:07:59.412
-More with Jim Parsons
after the break, everybody.

